    "id": "tipjet",
    "name": "Tip-prop heli",
    "blurb": "2.6 kg camera helicopter with a 1.5 m rotor driven by two 5-inch props out at the blades — no tail rotor, because tip drive reacts on the air and not on the airframe. The pedal is a motor on the mast, so it steers on the rotor’s inertia and spends headspeed doing it. A derotated camera rides the hub.",
    "notes": "Not a measurement of anything: a design worked out from one constraint. A propeller stops pulling at its pitch speed, so a rotor driven by props at its tips has to turn slowly — 45 m/s at the tip here against a 74 m/s pitch speed — and a slow rotor only makes thrust through area. That gives a big, lightly loaded disc with motor pods out near the tips, which is why it glides twice as far as a 450 and autorotates at under 3 m/s. Yaw is a 1.2 N·m motor across the mast bearing, reacting against the rotor, powered off the hub pack along with the derotator and the flight controller. Correct the all-up weight first; everything else follows the rotor.",
